C. Post-Experimental Data

The post-experimental data included (1) a preference ranking ranking of feedback modalities, (2) perceived haptic strength, and (3) qualitative data from the open post-experimental questions. Fig. 7 depicts the frequency of rank assignments for the different feedback modalities. Based on a Kruskal Wallis test, the modalities differed regarding their ranking (χ 2 = 27.43, df = 3, p < 0.01). The visual-only condition was least preferred. More than half of the participants preferred the multisensory modality ( Haptic and Audio present ). The mean perceived haptic strength was M haptic = 4.14 ( SD = 1.57). Three participants reported they did not consciously feel haptic feedback during the tasks. The verbal responses were categorized using categories that were established in our previous work [38] .
